#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078208 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6803a7be-e0e9-5f19-bd0b-711f49853170" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given Data or Tables Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "98fdea06-a5ad-5d22-b928-ef893424e447" :question/math-academy-id "e-158" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.7$ | $2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.2) & ≈\\frac{f(0.2+0.1)-f(0.2-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.3)-f(0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.7-1.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078209 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "62934fda-d972-50d5-acdb-e1f7b2fd63f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-159" :question/problem "![](images/53/5355859a0307e46ae84d12bc5e0b183d72458972c8a417fb6d9296b4769ea078.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{g(x+h) - g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+4)-g(4-4)}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(8)-g(0)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-10)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078212 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "d405fda1-1111-56db-ae34-4c0f257852f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-160" :question/problem "A car engine is being heated from a temperature of about $-10^{\\circ}.$ The temperature of the engine after $t$ minutes is given by $T(t).$  The graph of $T(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe78a78911e2df9cdb488ec57b0e0a34bb56e18b57303d895dc8013b64a4afc3.png)\n\nApproximate the rate at which the temperature of the engine increases when $t = 3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nT'(t) \\approx \\dfrac{T(t+h) - T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{T(3+1)-T(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(4)-T(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-30}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of temperature increase is approximately $15^{\\circ}$ per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6803a7be-e0e9-5f19-bd0b-711f49853170" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given Data or Tables Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "167457a9-b6b4-5440-b129-2f0689b6e02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281898} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6ae62d-82cc-507a-a390-da0ec1363bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282109} {:answer/id #uuid "30d44fd4-c8bf-5a7d-8db2-4407697e9981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282125} {:answer/id #uuid "534f1b55-dd7e-54a5-b639-76254d4ef738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282400} {:answer/id #uuid "b830ca2d-56fe-5d6d-b7b4-790849ecdebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "534f1b55-dd7e-54a5-b639-76254d4ef738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282400} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3db73c-bd8b-5985-8c46-305bfc25d447" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a448f94c-9203-5aa7-907f-0bb1a9165bbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-170649" :question/problem "Given that $f(2.9)=5.91$ and $f(3.1)=7.11,$ find an approximation for ${f}^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3$ with the step size $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+0.1)-f(3-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3.1)-f(2.9)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.11-5.91}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7e2085a-e531-5baf-8fe5-1d1d7a6ad9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e2085a-e531-5baf-8fe5-1d1d7a6ad9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296148} :answer-field/id #uuid "8369a49e-b4e6-517f-b327-76c2f9620e9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4894e4a4-bc69-5cbe-8eef-c136c4c17945" :question/math-academy-id "q-314898" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d27b0d70171683240fd44c8bbbcc8cb46ce0f0f216533a8061d31a991cce4e14.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=1$ and $x=3.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+1)-f(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47fdd234-4281-5a38-aa51-22a8934d48a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba96510-3aad-52e6-933c-3020fe0f7265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba96510-3aad-52e6-933c-3020fe0f7265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316180} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7d58ba-9fa1-5287-b687-96086e2fe893" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe82b330-59ab-5f93-9994-eddddff07551" :question/math-academy-id "q-344023" :question/problem "![](images/56/5663e7c59a9f42b31ebadd9abb54e36c604526c00334f840014a674c5b6dcdb8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=3.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=3$ and $x=9.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$ with step size $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{f(6+3)-f(6-3)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(9)-f(3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24265d78-819c-5988-b582-ea8cc29f061c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330259} {:answer/id #uuid "46912e59-1bd4-5051-b1a0-dba014f012f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.05\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330586} {:answer/id #uuid "8c562598-41b8-5c48-964d-0505e00da5b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331196} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d384e4-4ea2-55f9-94d9-867acdced7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331406} {:answer/id #uuid "a793588b-2a9a-563b-a526-fe17cec1ff70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24265d78-819c-5988-b582-ea8cc29f061c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330259} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d4a9e5-c745-5774-b2c2-c53d83fc4b40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c6e93bc-4fb5-52a4-975a-8dfbc53642f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51402" :question/problem "Sam wanted to estimate the rate of weight growth of his new puppy. He took note of its weight every week, for $7$ weeks. The results are shown in the table below, where $W(t)$ is the puppy's weight (measured in pounds) after $t$ weeks from birth.\n\n| **$t$** | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ | $14$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $W(t)$ | $9$ | $10.1$ | $11.3$ | $12.4$ | $13.5$ | $14.6$ | $15.8$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the puppy's rate of weight growth $12$ weeks after birth using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=12$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(0.2) & ≈\\frac{W(12+1)-W(12-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(13)-W(11)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14.6-12.4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.2}{2} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of weight growth at the instant of time $t=12$ is $1.1\\,\\text{lb}/\\text{week}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a00187-cbc3-5317-b9e6-87f2abfe2bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330126} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9de2d0-f9be-5cb4-96dd-ac8265f872ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330359} {:answer/id #uuid "8be85c09-ef47-5f5f-a842-3cc277864a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331191} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd28547-b0d9-5df0-a990-031060b1d5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331628} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe7849e-d032-5002-9336-c465f7fd494c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e9de2d0-f9be-5cb4-96dd-ac8265f872ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330359} :answer-field/id #uuid "3286ba04-ae3e-5cab-8f4c-55cb8cefd221" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14f1836e-031c-5d94-96ad-23aaab157b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-51405" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e11260301be8c0bb19a9fd47ffbbf71cc5fa4d94dbeae57af934b76fb17f0e1.png)\n\nA particle has a speed of $v(t)$ meters per second after $t$ seconds. The graph of $v(t)$ is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of speed when $t=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{v}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{v(t+h)-g(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{v(3+1)-v(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(4)-v(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of speed at the instant of time $t=2$ is approximately $1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"}]}
